.TH WFAS 1 "2026" "1.0.0" "WiFi Audio Streaming"
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH NAME
.\"
wfas \- stream audio over a local Wi\-Fi network
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\"
.B wfas
.RB [ --gui | --cli ]
.RB [ --mode
.IR server | client | discover ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B wfas
.B control
.IR volume " <0\-100> | mute | unmute | stop | status"
.br
.B wfas
.B config
.IR list | get | set | path | edit | reset | export | import
.br
.B wfas
.B pair
.IR invite | regenerate | "connect <link>" | "inspect <link>" | "encode <text>" | status | off | register | unregister
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\"
.B wfas
transmits audio from one device (server) to one or more receivers (clients)
on the same local network using the WFAS protocol.
It also supports RTP, HTTP, DLNA and Snapcast streaming as optional additional
protocols.
.PP
Run without arguments,
.B wfas
prints its help and exits.
Use
.B --cli
(or
.BR --server )
to start in CLI mode without loading any graphical interface,
making it suitable for use on headless systems such as Raspberry Pi or in
automation scripts.
.PP
When a display is available,
.B --gui
opens the full graphical interface.
Combining
.B --gui
with
.B --mode
opens the GUI and immediately starts the corresponding operation,
as if the user had clicked the Start button.
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH MODES
.\"
.TP
.B server
Capture audio from the system's default audio output (or a virtual cable)
and stream it to connected clients.
This is the default mode when no
.B --mode
flag is provided.
In unicast the server accepts
.B one
client at a time: while a session is active it stops announcing itself on the
network and answers any other device with
.BR WFAS_BUSY ,
leaving the running session untouched.
In multicast there is no such limit.
.TP
.B client
Receive audio from a server and play it on a local output device.
If the chosen server is already streaming to another device in unicast, the
connection attempt fails immediately with
.I "That server is already streaming to another device"
instead of waiting for the timeout.
If
.B --server
is omitted,
.B wfas
scans the network for available servers and connects automatically
when only one is found, or presents an interactive list otherwise.
.TP
.B discover
Scan the network and print available servers.
With
.B --watch
the scan continues until the process is interrupted.
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH OPTIONS
.\"
.SS Entry point
.TP
.B --gui
Open the graphical interface.
.TP
.B --cli
Start in CLI mode (explicit).
.\"
.SS Mode selection
.TP
\fB--mode\fR \fIserver\fR|\fIclient\fR|\fIdiscover\fR
Select the operating mode.
.\"
.SS Server options
.TP
\fB--port\fR \fIn\fR
WFAS streaming port (default:
.IR 9090 ).
.TP
\fB--mic-port\fR \fIn\fR
Port used to receive microphone audio from clients (default:
.IR 9092 ).
.TP
.B --multicast
Enable multicast transmission.
Automatically set when
.B --rtp
or
.B --http
is used.
.TP
.B --rtp
Enable RTP side-channel.
Allows any RTP-capable player (e.g. VLC) to receive the audio stream.
Implies
.BR --multicast .
.TP
\fB--rtp-port\fR \fIn\fR
RTP destination port (default:
.IR 9094 ).
.TP
.B --http
Enable HTTP streaming (AAC chunked + Opus WebSocket).
Implies
.BR --multicast .
.TP
\fB--http-port\fR \fIn\fR
HTTP server port (default:
.IR 8080 ).
.TP
.B --http-safari
Enable the Safari-compatible AAC stream on the HTTP server.
Implies
.BR --http ,
which in turn forces multicast.
.TP
.B --snapcast
Act as a Snapcast server for synchronised multiroom playback.
Any snapclient on the network (Raspberry Pi, ESP32, Home Assistant, the
Snapcast mobile apps) can join and stay in sync.
Implies
.BR --multicast ,
so the native WFAS session can keep serving other clients at the same time.
.TP
\fB--snapcast-port\fR \fIn\fR
Snapcast audio stream port (default:
.IR 1704 ).
.TP
\fB--snapcast-control-port\fR \fIn\fR
Snapcast JSON-RPC control port (default:
.IR 1705 ).
Clients such as Home Assistant and the official Snapcast apps use this port to
manage groups, per-client volume and latency.
.TP
\fB--snapcast-codec\fR \fIcodec\fR
One of
.IR pcm ", " flac " or " opus
(default:
.IR pcm ).
.I flac
roughly halves the bandwidth at the cost of about 25 ms of encoding delay.
.I opus
requires 48000 Hz stereo 16 bit input; when the audio settings do not match,
the server falls back to
.IR pcm .
.TP
\fB--snapcast-chunk\fR \fIn\fR
Chunk size in milliseconds:
.IR 10 ", " 20 ", " 40 " or " 60
(default:
.IR 20 ).
.TP
\fB--snapcast-buffer\fR \fIn\fR
Playback buffer advertised to clients, in milliseconds (default:
.IR 1000 ).
Higher values survive worse networks, lower values react faster.
.TP
\fB--snapcast-name\fR \fIname\fR
Stream identifier advertised to clients (default:
.IR default ).
.TP
.B --sdp
Print the SDP session descriptor to standard output when the server starts.
Only meaningful with
.BR --rtp .
.TP
\fB--sdp-out\fR \fIpath\fR
Write the SDP session descriptor to
.I path
in addition to printing it.
.\"
.SS Client options
.TP
\fB--connect\fR \fIip\fR
IP address of the server to connect to.
Implies client mode when no explicit mode is given.
If omitted,
.B wfas
performs automatic discovery.
.TP
\fB--output\fR \fIname\fR
Name (or substring) of the audio output device to use.
Uses the system default when omitted.
To list available devices run
.B wfas --mode discover --json
and look at
.IR serverAudioSettings .
.\"
.SS Microphone options (client-to-server return channel)
These options send the client's microphone back to the server (talkback),
independently of the main server-to-client audio stream.
.TP
.B --mic
Enable microphone return.
.TP
\fB--mic-input\fR \fIname\fR
Name (or substring) of the microphone device.
Uses the system default when omitted.
.TP
\fB--mic-routing\fR \fImode\fR
How the microphone is routed on the server side.
.RS
.TP
.B mix
Mix microphone into the outgoing audio stream (default when
.B --mic
is used).
.TP
.B virtual
Route microphone to a virtual output device (e.g. CABLE Input).
.TP
.B off
Disable microphone routing.
.RE
.\"
.SS Audio options
.TP
\fB--volume\fR \fIn\fR
Initial volume as a percentage from 0 to 100 (default:
.IR 100 ).
.TP
.B --mute
Start with audio muted.
.\"
.SS Runtime control
These subcommands communicate with a running
.B wfas
instance via an IPC socket and exit immediately.
.TP
\fBcontrol volume\fR \fIn\fR
Set the volume to
.I n
percent (0\-100).
.TP
.B control mute
Mute the audio.
.TP
.B control unmute
Unmute the audio.
.TP
.B control stop
Stop the current stream without closing the process.
.TP
.B control status
Print the current status as JSON, including mode, volume, mute state,
port, protocols enabled, uptime, and PID.
.\"
.SS Configuration
These subcommands read and write the persistent settings file
.RB ( config.json )
shared by the CLI and the GUI, then exit.
Changes take effect the next time a server or client starts, or the next time
the GUI is opened.
Add
.B --json
to any of them for machine-readable output.
.TP
.B config list
Print every setting and its current value, grouped by section.
.TP
\fBconfig get\fR \fIkey\fR
Print the value of one setting, for example
.BR audio.sampleRate .
.TP
\fBconfig set\fR \fIkey\fR \fIvalue\fR
Change one setting and save it.
The value is validated against the setting's type and range.
.TP
.B config path
Print the absolute path of the settings file resolved for the current system.
.TP
.B config edit
Open the settings file in an editor.
Uses
.B $VISUAL
or
.B $EDITOR
when set, otherwise the operating system's default handler for the file.
.TP
.B config reset
Restore every setting to its default value.
.TP
\fBconfig export\fR [\fIfile\fR]
Write the current settings to
.I file
as JSON, or to standard output when
.I file
is omitted.
.TP
\fBconfig import\fR \fIfile\fR
Load a JSON settings file and make it the active configuration.
.\"
.SS QR pairing
.B wfas pair
hands a receiver everything it needs in one shot: address, port, and a freshly
generated 256\-bit key.
The key is never typed by the user and never travels on the wire: the invite
carries it, and the mutual HMAC challenge\-response of
.B --protocol
proves it.
Invites are valid for 120 seconds.
.PP
Generating an invite switches this device to key authentication and stores the
generated key in the settings file, leaving any manually typed key untouched so
that turning pairing off restores it.
When a
.B wfas
instance is already running, the invite is produced by that instance over the IPC
channel, so it matches the live session; otherwise it is produced offline and
saved to the configuration, ready for the next
.BR "wfas --server" .
.TP
.B pair invite
Generate an invite and draw it as a QR code on the terminal.
In multicast, an invite reuses the current generated key when there is one, so
inviting somebody does not disconnect the listeners already playing.
.TP
.B pair regenerate
Same, but always with a brand new key.
In multicast this evicts every listener still using the old key, which is the
point after handing the code to the wrong person.
Aliases:
.BR rekey ", " new-key .
.TP
\fBpair connect\fR \fIlink\fR
Join using an invite link, in either the
.B wifiaudio://pair?
or the
.B https://
form.
Starts the client with the key already applied.
Fails with a distinct message when the invite has expired, instead of reporting a
wrong key.
.TP
\fBpair inspect\fR \fIlink\fR
Decode a link and print what it contains, without connecting.
The key is masked unless
.B --show-key
is given.
Exits with status 1 when the invite has expired.
Alias:
.BR check .
.TP
\fBpair encode\fR \fItext\fR
Render arbitrary text as a QR code on the terminal.
.TP
.B pair status
Print the pairing state: whether QR pairing is on, whether the stored key was
generated or typed, and whether the
.B wifiaudio://
handler is registered with this system.
It also prints the command that scheme is actually registered to open. If that
line is missing, or does not end in
.BR \(dq%1\(dq ,
the handler is broken and the browser will appear to do nothing when the pairing
page tries to open the app: run
.B pair register
to repair it.
.TP
.B pair off
Turn QR pairing off and restore the manually typed key, if there was one.
.TP
.B pair register
Register the
.B wifiaudio://
scheme with the operating system, per user and without administrator rights
(Windows registry under
.BR HKCU ", "
a
.B .desktop
file on Linux; on macOS the scheme is declared in the bundle's
.BR Info.plist ", "
so nothing is needed).
.IP
You should never need this. Every run of
.B wfas
checks the registration in the background and rewrites it when it is missing
.I or when it points at a different executable
than the one running, so moving, reinstalling or updating the app repairs itself
on the next launch. A stale registration is worse than none: it still looks valid
and simply opens nothing. This subcommand exists to force that repair immediately
and to see it fail with a reason.
.IP
On Windows the registration is applied by importing a generated
.B .reg
file rather than by passing values to
.BR reg.exe .
The command to register is
.RB \(lq "\(dqC:\(rspath\(rsapp.exe\(dq \(dq%1\(dq" \(rq,
which contains both spaces and quotes; passing that as a single argument does not
survive Windows command-line quoting, and the value lands in the registry
truncated, without the
.B %1
that carries the invite. Inside a file the quoting never crosses a command line.
.TP
.B pair unregister
Remove that registration.
.TP
.B --watch
Only with
.BR "pair invite" :
keep the code on screen with a live countdown and renew it automatically when it
expires.
While watching, press
.B n
for a new invite,
.B r
for a new key,
.B k
to reveal or hide the key, and
.B q
to quit, each followed by Enter.
.TP
.B --json
Emit the invite as a single JSON object
.RB ( uri ", " deeplink ", " key ", " ip ", " port ", " mode ", " expires_at ", "
.BR expires_in ", " ttl ", " source )
instead of drawing anything.
.TP
.B --quiet
Print only the invite link, one line, nothing else.
Useful for piping into another QR renderer.
.\"
.SS QR rendering options
The terminal renderer prints one character per two vertical modules using the
half\-block characters U+2580 and U+2584, which corrects for the roughly 2:1
aspect ratio of a terminal cell.
An invite needs 61 columns, 65 with an IPv6 address, so it fits an 80\-column
window; when it does not, the code is replaced by a message saying how many
columns are missing, and the link is still printed.
It is 31 to 33 rows tall, so on a 24\-row terminal the code scrolls off: in that
case
.B --watch
keeps only the countdown on the bottom line instead of repainting the whole
panel in place.
.TP
.B --qr
Server mode only: generate a 256\-bit key, start the server with it, and print
the pairing QR once streaming is up.
It implies
.B --auth-mode key
and
.BR --encrypt ,
and therefore does not need
.BR --auth-key :
the whole point is that the code carries a key nobody has to type.
The key is generated before the server starts, so the very first packet on the
wire is already encrypted; an existing generated key is reused instead of being
rotated, so restarting the server does not evict listeners.
The interactive keys
.B p
(new invite) and
.B r
(new key) are then accepted alongside
.B q
and
.BR v .
.PP
.B --encrypt
on its own is rejected: ChaCha20\-Poly1305 needs a key, and a server started with
an empty one would authenticate nobody. Pass
.B --auth-key
or
.BR --qr .
.TP
.B --no-qr
Print only the link, without the ASCII art.
.TP
.B --plain
Use two characters per module instead of half\-blocks.
Needed when the terminal font lacks U+2580 and U+2584; it requires twice the
width.
Selected automatically when the locale and
.B file.encoding
suggest the terminal is not UTF\-8.
.TP
.B --invert
Invert the code, for terminals with a light background.
.TP
.B --show-key
Print the pairing key instead of masking it.
.\"
.SS Discover options
Discovery prints one row per server in a table with the columns
.BR HOST ", " IP ", " MODE " (RTP, HTTP, UDP, or UDP/M for multicast), and "
.B SECURITY
.RB ( None ", " Authentication ", or " Encryption ).
.B Encryption
is reported only for key-authenticated servers that also encrypt the audio;
a server that merely authorizes or asks for a key reports
.BR Authentication .
.TP
.B --watch
Keep scanning continuously instead of exiting after 5 seconds.
.TP
.B --json
Output results as newline-delimited JSON objects instead of the table.
Each object carries the same fields plus
.BR port ", " multicast ", and " protocols .
.\"
.SS Link options (transport and address family)
.TP
.B --usb
Stream over the USB cable instead of Wi\-Fi.
Enable USB tethering on the Android phone: the phone acts as gateway and the app
locates the resulting network interface on its own, so the wire protocol is
unchanged \(em only the path differs.
Jitter is far steadier than on Wi\-Fi, which is what makes a shorter buffer safe.
Not available on macOS, which has no built\-in RNDIS driver, so Android USB
tethering never comes up there.
Overrides the stored
.B net.usbMode
for this run only.
.TP
.B --no-usb
Force the USB link off even when enabled in the configuration.
.TP
\fB--usb-latency\fR \fIms\fR
Jitter buffer applied only while the USB link is up (default:
.IR 20 ,
range 5\-120).
The Wi\-Fi buffer selected with
.B --latency
is left untouched, so the two transports keep independent depths.
Implies
.BR --usb .
.TP
\fB--wfas-mode\fR \fImode\fR
Controls when this device serves the native WFAS protocol:
.RS
.TP
.B always
Always, both over Wi\-Fi and over the cable.
.TP
.B not-on-usb
Served over Wi\-Fi, but suppressed as soon as the USB link comes up (default).
Useful to keep the phone from being reachable over the network while it is
wired.
.TP
.B off
Never served over Wi\-Fi; only the cable and the other protocols remain.
.RE
.IP
With
.B off
and neither
.BR --rtp ", " --http
nor a USB link there is nothing left to serve, and the server refuses to start
rather than running silently.
.TP
.BR --ip4 ", " --ipv4
Restrict the app to IPv4: bind
.IR 0.0.0.0 ,
announce and listen on the
.I 239.255.0.1
multicast group only.
.TP
.BR --ip6 ", " --ipv6
Restrict the app to IPv6: use the
.I [ff02::5746]
multicast group only.
.IP
With neither flag the app is dual\-stack: it binds the wildcard address, joins
both groups, and for each peer keeps the most usable address it hears \(em a
routable IPv4 or IPv6 wins over a link\-local one.
Force a family only to work around a broken network; on an IPv6\-only LAN the
automatic behaviour already selects IPv6.
.\"
.SS Global options
.TP
\fB--interface\fR \fIname\fR
Name of the network interface to use (default:
.IR Auto ,
which picks the active non-virtual interface automatically).
.TP
\fB--config\fR \fIpath\fR
Load settings from
.I path
instead of the default settings store.
Useful when running multiple instances with different configurations.
.TP
.B --json
Emit all output as newline-delimited JSON.
Useful for scripting and monitoring pipelines.
.TP
.B --quiet
Suppress informational output.
Only errors are written to standard error.
.TP
.BR --legacy-engine ", " --no-native-engine
(Server only.)
Use the legacy FFmpeg audio grabber instead of the native C audio engine.
.PP
The native engine is the default on all three platforms:
.RS
.TP
.B Windows
WASAPI exclusive loopback \[em] no virtual driver required.
.TP
.B macOS
ScreenCaptureKit \[em] system audio capture.
.TP
.B Linux
PulseAudio/PipeWire via
.BR dlopen (3),
automatically mutes the default speaker sink
while streaming and restores it on exit.
.RE
.PP
Use
.B --legacy-engine
on Linux when PulseAudio or PipeWire is not available
(e.g.\& ALSA-only minimal systems), or as a fallback if the native
engine fails to initialise.
.B --no-native-engine
is a legacy alias for the same flag.
.TP
\fB--viz\fR [\fItheme\fR]
Draw an animated ASCII spectrum histogram of the audio being streamed, plus a
per-channel level meter. The optional
.I theme
is either a hex colour (e.g.\&
.BR "#1e88e5" )
which recolours the whole view from that seed, or the literal
.BR rainbow
for an animated hue sweep. Press
.B q
to quit and
.B v
to adjust the volume without leaving the view.
.TP
\fB--groove\fR [\fIamount\fR]
(Requires
.BR --viz .)
Adaptive spectrum instead of raw levels. A plain FFT display is dominated by
low frequencies, which carry most of the energy in nearly all music: the bass
bars sit pinned at full scale and everything else collapses into a static
blob. With
.B --groove
each band is judged relative to its own context rather than in absolute terms:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
each band is compared with its frequency neighbours, so a note that pokes out
of its region is amplified while broadband noise stays flat;
.IP \(bu 3
a slow envelope per band is subtracted, so the constant part of the spectrum
stops dominating and the low bars no longer sit pinned at full scale.
.PP
The net effect is that the display tracks the melody and the rhythm rather than
the raw energy. Everything is relative, so silence stays silent: a gate fades
the processing out when there is no signal, and no noise is invented.
.PP
The optional
.I amount
is
.BR soft ", " normal " or " hard
(equivalently a number from 0 to 160, where 100 is
.BR normal ).
Press
.B g
inside the visualizer to toggle the effect on and off while it runs, which is
the easiest way to see what it does.
.RE
.TP
.BR --monitor ", " --listen
(Requires
.BR --viz .)
Do not start a server: just visualize the system audio through loopback
capture, leaving the system volume untouched.
.TP
.B --protocol
Print an explanation of the WFAS v2 wire protocol (packet format, control
messages, version negotiation) and exit.
.TP
.B --licenses
Print the third-party open-source licenses (including the FFmpeg/LGPL notice)
and exit. See also the
.B LICENSING
section below.
.TP
.B --help
Print usage information and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Print the version string and exit.
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH IPC CONTROL
.\"
When
.B wfas
starts it creates a file
.IR /tmp/wfas-<pid>.port
containing the TCP port number of its local IPC socket.
The
.B control
subcommand reads this file, connects to the socket, and sends a JSON command.
.PP
.B pair invite
uses the same socket: when an instance is running it asks that process to mint
the invite, so the key and the multicast epoch match the live session rather than
a stale copy read from the settings file.
With no instance running it falls back to generating the invite locally.
.PP
If multiple
.B wfas
instances are running simultaneously, the control command targets the most
recently started instance.
A future
.B --pid
option will allow targeting a specific instance by PID.
.PP
The IPC socket listens only on the loopback interface and is not accessible
from other machines.
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH FILES
.\"
.TP
.I ~/.config/wfas/config.json
User settings (Linux; honors
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/wfas/config.json
User settings (macOS).
.TP
.I %APPDATA%\ewfas\econfig.json
User settings (Windows).
.IP
The settings file is a single JSON document shared by the CLI and the GUI.
Inspect or edit it with
.B wfas config
(see COMMANDS), or override its location with
.BR --config .
Run
.B wfas config path
to print the exact path resolved for the current system.
.TP
.I /tmp/wfas-<pid>.port
IPC port file created by each running instance.
Deleted automatically on exit.
.TP
.I /tmp/stream.sdp
RTP session descriptor written when
.B --rtp
is active.
Used by external players.
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH EXIT STATUS
.\"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error (bad arguments, port conflict, device not found, etc.).
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH EXAMPLES
.\"
Start a CLI server on the default port:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --cli
.RE
.PP
Print the WFAS v2 protocol reference:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --protocol
.RE
.PP
Serve over the USB cable, with an aggressive buffer:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --server --usb --usb-latency 10
.RE
.PP
Receive on an IPv6\-only network:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --client --ip6
.RE
.PP
List servers with transport and address family:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas discover --json
.RE
.PP
Start a server with RTP enabled and print the SDP to stdout:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --mode server --rtp --sdp
.RE
.PP
Pipe the SDP directly into VLC:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --mode server --rtp --sdp | vlc -
.RE
.PP
Write the SDP to a file for later use:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --mode server --rtp --sdp-out /tmp/stream.sdp
.RE
.PP
Connect as a client to a specific server:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --connect 192.168.1.42
.RE
.PP
Connect and specify the output device:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --connect 192.168.1.42 --output "Built-in Audio"
.RE
.PP
Discover servers and output JSON (useful for scripts):
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --mode discover --json
.RE
.PP
Keep the discovery running and monitor in real time:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --mode discover --watch
.RE
.PP
Open the GUI and start the server immediately:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --gui --mode server --multicast
.RE
.PP
Open the GUI and connect to a specific server immediately:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas --gui --mode client --connect 192.168.1.42
.RE
.PP
Set volume to 60% on a running instance:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas control volume 60
.RE
.PP
Check the status of the running instance:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas control status
.RE
.PP
Run as a systemd service on Raspberry Pi (example unit file):
.PP
.RS
.nf
[Unit]
Description=WiFi Audio Streaming Server
After=network.target sound.target

[Service]
ExecStart=/opt/wfas/bin/wfas --mode server --multicast
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
User=pi

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
.fi
.RE
.PP
Monitor volume from a shell script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
while true; do
    wfas control status --json | jq -r .volume
    sleep 5
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start a server and show its pairing code, refreshed as it expires:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wfas --server --multicast --qr
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show a live pairing code for an already running server:
.PP
.RS
.B wfas pair invite --watch
.RE
.PP
Hand the invite to another tool instead of drawing it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wfas pair invite --quiet | qrencode -t ANSIUTF8
wfas pair invite --json | jq -r .key
.fi
.RE
.PP
Join from a link, and check it first if in doubt:
.PP
.RS
.nf
wfas pair inspect "$LINK" && wfas pair connect "$LINK"
.fi
.RE
.\"
.\" ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.SH NOTES
.\"
On Linux the native C audio engine uses
.BR dlopen (3)
to load
.B libpulse-simple.so.0
at runtime.
No link-time dependency is required; the
.B libaudio_engine.so
bundled in the package works on any distribution.
If
.B libpulse-simple
is not installed, start the server with
.B --legacy-engine
to use the FFmpeg backend instead, and install
.B pulseaudio-utils
(Debian/RPi OS) or
.B pipewire-pulseaudio
(Fedora/Arch) to enable the native engine.
.PP
On Windows, some firewalls block multicast packets.
If clients cannot discover the server, try disabling multicast with
.B --multicast
omitted and providing the server IP explicitly via
.B --connect
on the client side.
.PP
A unicast server is bound to the address of its connected client for the whole
session: control messages arriving from any other address are discarded, so a
third device on the LAN cannot terminate someone else's session.
To serve several listeners at once, use multicast.
.PP
The
.B --sdp
flag generates an SDP descriptor based on the configured audio settings.
The payload type is L16 (raw PCM) over RTP.
Most players including VLC, ffplay, and GStreamer support this format.
.\"
